  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0031\" contains five tables. The primary key of each table is indicated by the \"id\" column.\nThe \"auth_user\" table is used to store user information. It includes fields such as \"password\", \"last_login\", \"is_superuser\", \"username\", \"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"email\", \"is_staff\", \"is_active\", and \"date_joined\". The \"id\" field is the primary key and is an integer.\nThe \"oauth2_provider_accesstoken\" table is used to store access tokens generated by the OAuth2 provider. It includes fields such as \"token\", \"expires\", \"scope\", \"application_id\", \"user_id\", \"created\", \"updated\", and \"source_refresh_token_id\". The \"id\" field is the primary key and is a big integer. It references the \"oauth2_provider_application\" and \"oauth2_provider_refreshtoken\" tables via foreign keys.\nThe \"oauth2_provider_application\" table is used to store OAuth2 applications. It includes fields such as \"client_id\", \"redirect_uris\", \"client_type\", \"authorization_grant_type\", \"client_secret\", \"name\", \"user_id\", \"skip_authorization\", \"created\", and \"updated\". The \"id\" field is the primary key and is a big integer. It references the \"auth_user\" table via a foreign key.\nThe \"oauth2_provider_grant\" table is used to store grant information for OAuth2. It includes fields such as \"code\", \"expires\", \"redirect_uri\", \"scope\", \"application_id\", \"user_id\", \"created\", \"updated\", \"code_challenge\", and \"code_challenge_method\". The \"id\" field is the primary key and is a big integer. It references the \"oauth2_provider_application\" and \"auth_user\" tables via foreign keys.\nThe \"oauth2_provider_refreshtoken\" table is used to store refresh tokens for OAuth2. It includes fields such as \"token\", \"access_token_id\", \"application_id\", \"user_id\", \"created\", \"updated\", and \"revoked\". The \"id\" field is the primary key and is a big integer. It references the \"oauth2_provider_application\", \"auth_user\", and \"oauth2_provider_accesstoken\" tables via foreign keys.\nThis schema is designed to handle user authentication, OAuth2 token generation, and granting of access tokens, all of which are crucial for secure communication in a web application.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an OAuth2 client and server for Django. This system will provide a comprehensive solution, including endpoints, data handling, and logic, to integrate OAuth2 authentication into Django projects, enabling secure API access and token management.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n- Implement OAuth2 authentication to protect web APIs within Django applications.\n- Provide an OAuth2 authorization server for token management.\n- Ensure adherence to RFC standards using OAuthLib for compliance and security.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Integrating OAuth2 authentication into Django projects.\n- API Consumers: Accessing secured web APIs using OAuth2 tokens.\n- System Administrators: Managing OAuth2 clients, tokens, and user authentication.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Create multiple OAuth2 endpoints for authorization, token issuance, and token revocation.\n- Enable developers to manage applications (clients) with attributes like client ID, secret, and redirect URIs.\n- Handle OAuth2 grant types such as authorization code, implicit, and client credentials.\n- Allow users to authenticate and authorize third-party applications to access their data.\n- Utilize access and refresh tokens for session management, supporting token expiration and renewal.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Ensure that the system is RFC-compliant for standardization and security.\n- Provide a scalable solution capable of handling multiple simultaneous API requests.\n- Utilize secure methods for storing and transmitting sensitive information, such as client secrets and user credentials.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0031.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0031\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_superuser` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `username` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(254) NOT NULL,\n  `is_staff` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `date_joined` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_accesstoken` (\n  `id` bigint(20) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expires` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` bigint(20) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `source_refresh_token_id` bigint(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_accesstoken_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_application` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_accesstoken_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_accesstoken_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`source_refresh_token_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_application` (\n  `id` bigint(20) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `client_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `redirect_uris` text NOT NULL,\n  `client_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `authorization_grant_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `client_secret` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `skip_authorization` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_application_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_grant` (\n  `id` bigint(20) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expires` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `redirect_uri` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` bigint(20) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `code_challenge` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `code_challenge_method` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_grant_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_application` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_grant_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken` (\n  `id` bigint(20) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `access_token_id` bigint(20) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` bigint(20) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `revoked` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`access_token_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_accesstoken` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_application` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
